WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.294 --> 00:00:06.126
Marius entered Cosette’s garden each night by pushing aside one of the bars in the gate.

2
00:00:06.127 --> 00:00:13.951
For more than a month, they met among the trees and shrubbery. They told each other about their families and their pasts.

3
00:00:13.952 --> 00:00:22.277
Marius had fallen so madly in love, he had temporarily forgotten all about that strange night in the Thenardiers’ apartment.

4
00:00:22.278 --> 00:00:28.126
He did not know at night what he had eaten for breakfast or what he had done all day.

5
00:00:28.127 --> 00:00:34.356
He lived only for the hours when he was able to see Cosette. Then he was in heaven.

6
00:00:34.357 --> 00:00:41.110
Jean Valjean suspected nothing. Cosette, though less distracted than Marius, was cheerful.

7
00:00:41.111 --> 00:00:45.499
She was happy, and that was enough to make Valjean happy.

8
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:51.172
Marius always arrived after Valjean was in bed, so the two men never met.

9
00:00:51.173 --> 00:00:57.094
Marius never entered the house. He sat with Cosette in the garden and held her hand.

10
00:00:57.095 --> 00:01:07.086
One night in early June 1832, Marius arrived and found that Cosette was sad. She had been weeping and her eyes were red.

11
00:01:07.087 --> 00:01:10.411
"What’s the matter?" asked Marius in alarm.

12
00:01:10.412 --> 00:01:14.324
She sat on a bench and he took his place beside her.

13
00:01:14.325 --> 00:01:20.184
"My father told me this morning that I must prepare to go away."

14
00:01:20.185 --> 00:01:21.737
"What do you mean?"

15
00:01:21.738 --> 00:01:31.554
"He said I should tidy up my affairs and be ready to leave Paris in a week. And oh, Marius, this is the worst part. . . ."

16
00:01:31.555 --> 00:01:38.721
Here Cosette had to stop so she wouldn’t begin to cry. "He said that perhaps we would move to England!"

17
00:01:38.722 --> 00:01:41.761
"That’s horrible! When would you come back?"

18
00:01:41.762 --> 00:01:43.388
"He didn’t say."

19
00:01:43.389 --> 00:01:48.037
"So will you go to England?" asked Marius.

20
00:01:48.038 --> 00:01:51.713
Cosette’s eyes were full of pain and confusion.

21
00:01:51.714 --> 00:01:59.784
"Why do you ask me that? If my father leaves for England, of course I must go with him."

22
00:01:59.785 --> 00:02:06.109
Marius shuddered. "This is all so sudden. Is there anything we can do to stop him?"

23
00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:14.268
Cosette shook her head, but then she began to smile. "There is one thing you could do. Follow me to England!"

24
00:02:14.269 --> 00:02:21.682
"Go with you? Are you insane?" Marius leaped up. "I don’t have money for a passport, never mind a ticket.

25
00:02:21.683 --> 00:02:28.998
You only see me at night, so you don’t realize how shabby my clothes are. If you saw me by day, you’d think I was a beggar."

26
00:02:28.999 --> 00:02:38.165
Marius leaned against a tree with his eyes closed. He stood a long time like that until he heard a small, stifled sound.

27
00:02:38.166 --> 00:02:42.991
It was Cosette sobbing. He fell on his knees in front of her.

28
00:02:42.992 --> 00:02:46.562
"Don’t weep," he said. "Do you love me?"

29
00:02:46.563 --> 00:02:50.991
"You know that I do. Do you love me?"

30
00:02:50.992 --> 00:02:57.601
"Cosette, I’ve never given my word of honor to anyone because that’s such a serious thing to do.

31
00:02:57.602 --> 00:03:01.983
But I swear that if you go away, I shall die!"

32
00:03:01.984 --> 00:03:05.221
Cosette was so shocked that she stopped crying.

33
00:03:05.222 --> 00:03:13.142
"Now listen," said Marius. "Don’t expect me tomorrow night. I have something I must do, but I will return here the next night.

34
00:03:13.143 --> 00:03:17.588
In the meantime you should know my address in case you need to get in touch with me.

35
00:03:17.589 --> 00:03:22.808
I live with my friend, Courfeyrac, at 16 Rue de la Verrerie."

36
00:03:22.809 --> 00:03:26.967
He took out his penknife and scratched the address on the garden wall.

37
00:03:26.968 --> 00:03:31.959
"I will wait for you," said Cosette, taking his hands in hers.

38
00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:36.093
The next evening Marius set out for his grandfather’s house.

39
00:03:36.094 --> 00:03:41.118
For four years Monsieur Gillenormand had hoped that Marius would return.

40
00:03:41.119 --> 00:03:46.777
But the old man was still stunned when a servant announced that Marius wished to see him.

41
00:03:46.778 --> 00:03:49.840
"Show him in," said Monsieur Gillenormand.

42
00:03:49.842 --> 00:03:52.444
The young man entered the room.

43
00:03:52.445 --> 00:03:58.336
Gillenormand didn’t know what to say, so he said rather abruptly, "What do you want?"

44
00:03:58.337 --> 00:03:59.690
"Monsieur . . ."

45
00:03:59.691 --> 00:04:04.102
Monsieur? Gillenormand couldn’t believe what he was hearing.

46
00:04:04.103 --> 00:04:10.761
Marius should rush to him and embrace him, not stand across the room, calling him "monsieur."

47
00:04:10.762 --> 00:04:17.491
"Did you come to beg my pardon?" interrupted Gillenormand. "Have you realized you were wrong?"

48
00:04:17.492 --> 00:04:18.976
"No, monsieur."

49
00:04:18.977 --> 00:04:25.253
"Then what do you want from me?" exclaimed Gillenormand in a voice filled with grief and rage.

50
00:04:25.254 --> 00:04:33.721
"Father, I know that my presence here is upsetting you. But I came to ask only one thing of you and then I will go away."

51
00:04:33.722 --> 00:04:41.665
That one word, "father," was all Gillenormand needed to hear. "What can I do for you, my son?"

52
00:04:41.666 --> 00:04:47.745
Marius explained that he wanted his grandfather’s permission to marry Cosette.

53
00:04:47.746 --> 00:04:54.467
Gillenormand looked him up and down. It was obvious from the young man’s clothes that he didn’t have any money.

54
00:04:54.468 --> 00:04:57.374
"How much money do you make as a lawyer?"

55
00:04:57.375 --> 00:05:00.792
"I don’t work as a lawyer," Marius replied.

56
00:05:00.793 --> 00:05:06.118
"So the girl is rich? She must have a large dowry," said Gillenormand.

57
00:05:06.119 --> 00:05:10.792
"Oh no, she doesn’t have any dowry. She’s an orphan like me."

58
00:05:10.793 --> 00:05:19.697
Gillenormand laughed. "Then, my dear boy, love this girl but don’t marry her. Keep her as your mistress."

59
00:05:19.698 --> 00:05:26.959
"Never!" said Marius. "You have insulted Cosette by even suggesting that. Good evening, monsieur."

60
00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:32.784
And with that, he turned and fled his grandfather’s house for the second time in his life.

61
00:05:32.785 --> 00:05:38.689
That same afternoon Jean Valjean was sitting behind a slope.

62
00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:46.022
He wore a workingman’s waistcoat, brown linen trousers, and a cap with a large visor that hid his face.

63
00:05:46.023 --> 00:05:51.609
Valjean was anxious about a number of things and needed time alone to think.

64
00:05:51.610 --> 00:05:55.813
A while ago he had spotted Thenardier near his house.

65
00:05:55.814 --> 00:06:00.911
Thanks to Valjean’s disguise as a workingman, the villain had not recognized him.

66
00:06:00.912 --> 00:06:08.831
Valjean had seen Thenardier again several times, and he was now certain that Thenardier was prowling about the neighborhood.

67
00:06:08.832 --> 00:06:16.339
This alone was enough to make Valjean consider leaving Paris. But there were other things preying on his mind.

68
00:06:16.340 --> 00:06:22.998
There was political unrest in Paris, which meant that the police were more alert than usual.

69
00:06:22.999 --> 00:06:27.648
Valjean feared that they might find him when they were arresting other people.

70
00:06:27.649 --> 00:06:33.545
So he had decided to leave France with Cosette, but he was worried about getting a passport.

71
00:06:33.546 --> 00:06:38.894
In addition, something strange had happened that very morning.

72
00:06:38.895 --> 00:06:46.426
Valjean had been walking in the garden at dawn when he came across a message: 16 Rue de la Verrerie

73
00:06:46.427 --> 00:06:49.045
. The words were scratched upon a

74
00:06:49.046 --> 00:06:52.984
wall. They looked quite fresh, so they must have been written

75
00:06:52.984 --> 00:06:59.624
overnight. But what was this? An address? A warning for him? A signal for

76
00:06:59.625 --> 00:07:04.521
others? Valjean had no idea, but it was obvious that someone had been in the

77
00:07:04.522 --> 00:07:12.513
garden. He remembered Cosette’s sighting of the shadow with the hat. It would probably be wise not to mention this latest mystery to

78
00:07:12.514 --> 00:07:18.233
her. In the midst of these thoughts, he sensed that someone was standing on the slope behind

79
00:07:18.234 --> 00:07:23.021
him. He was about to turn around when a piece of paper fell at his

80
00:07:23.022 --> 00:07:27.180
feet. Valjean jumped up just in time to see someone running

81
00:07:27.181 --> 00:07:35.172
away. This person was larger than a child but smaller than a man, and dressed in a gray smock and brown velvet

82
00:07:35.173 --> 00:07:42.333
pants. Valjean picked up the paper and read a message scrawled in large letters: Move out.

